"\kappa(x)" is defined in (9) as
\kappa(x) := \frac{\max_{\mathsf d(s)\neq y^\star(x)}\delta_\ell(s,x)}{\min_{\mathsf d(s)\neq y^\star(x)}\delta_\ell(s,x)}
(which, being a max/min over the same set, satisfies \kappa(x)\ge 1 when the denominator>0). The paper also states explicitly that "binary classification has \kappa(x)=1 provided \mathbb P(\Delta(X)>0)=1." (see equation (9) and the following sentences).

Theorem 1 (Sec. 3.2.3) and the explicit xi_{m,k} formulas immediately following it define thresholds involving factors 1/(1-\alpha) and 1/(1-\alpha^2): e.g. Theorem 1 requires R(f)-R^*\ge 2P(\kappa(X)>M)+(4\mathsf C_{\mathsf{MT}} e_m(M))^{1/(1-\alpha)}, and the paper defines
