"Theorem 1" and the explicit definition of \xi_{m,k} earlier in Section 3.2.3 use exponents with denominators (1-\alpha) and (1-\alpha^2). For example Theorem 1 gives a threshold term (4\mathsf C_{\mathsf{MT}} e_m(M))^{1/(1-\alpha)} (requiring division by 1-\alpha), and the paper defines

\xi_{m,k}:=\begin{cases}
\left(\frac{32\mathsf C_{\mathsf{MT}}^2}{m}\log\left(\frac{8(\mathsf C_{\varphi,1}+\mathsf C_{\varphi,2})}{\mathsf C_{\varphi,1}}\right)\right)^{1/(2(1-\alpha))}, & k=2,\\[4mm]
4\cdot\left(\frac{32\mathsf C_{\mathsf{MT}}^4}{m}\log\left(\frac{4k(\mathsf C_{\varphi,1}+\mathsf C_{\varphi,2})}{\mathsf C_{\varphi,1}}\right)\right)^{\alpha/(2(1-\alpha^2))}, & \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}

Both displayed exponents (1/(2(1-\alpha)) and \alpha/(2(1-\alpha^2))) become undefined (division by zero) at \alpha=1, so the earlier formulas do not support setting \alpha exactly equal to 1. (See Theorem 1 and the paragraph defining \xi_{m,k} in Section 3.2.3.)

